ints in 800: expected max gap ~800*log(40)/40 ≈ 74, min gaps near 0. This is a huge clue! The stars are evenly spaced horizontally! So their x positions likely follow a regular grid, and hidden data is in **y position** or **brightness/size**.\n\nYes! If x positions are systematically spaced ~20px, the stars might be generated as a row of stars with jitter, and one star per column for data. There are 40 stars across 800, spacing ~20. Maybe each star encodes a bit/character via vertical position. The y positions range 29-135 (mostly upper sky), which could encode 7 bits (0-127) per star! That's 40 characters. Aha! The y coordinate might directly encode ASCII or an index. y values: 29.5, 34.5, 44.5, 54.5, 69.5, 74.5, 86.4, 89.5, 94.5, 99.5, 104.5, 119.5, 124.5, 129.5, 134.5. Some repeated. If star y encodes a character, 40 stars -> 40 chars.\n\nSimilarly, x positions might encode data, but they are constrained to be evenly spread. The y values could be chosen from a set corresponding to letters. Let's examine y values: many at 29.5, 54.5, 99.5, 104.5, 124.5, 129.5... These might be positions in a *5x7 font*? Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the stars spell a message when plotted, and their x positions evenly spaced because text characters are evenly spaced! Each star could be a pixel/dot of a **pixel font** but only one star per column? No.\n\nActually if text is rendered as a bitmap and each \"on\" pixel becomes a star, x positions would be at multiples of character-cell width, and y positions at multiples of pixel height. The x values would be quantized to a grid (e.g., 20px spacing), exactly! And y values quantized to a grid too (e.g., 5px). Let's check: x values are 23.5, 40.5, 62.5, 78.5, 101.5, ... differences alternate 17,22,16,23,15,21,19,24. That's not exactly constant but could be due to each glyph's dot positions: if a 5x7 font is rendered at ~4-5 px per cell, x positions would be multiples of 4-5. The differences (15-24) are not multiples of a small grid unless columns